   "text": "The history of the printing press is not merely the history of a machine; it is the history of a revolution in human consciousness. Before the advent of movable type in fifteenth-century Europe, knowledge was a precious, scarce commodity, painstakingly preserved and guarded by a select few. The printing press shattered that monopoly, democratizing information and setting in motion a chain of events that would lead to the Reformation, the Scientific Revolution, the Enlightenment, and the modern world. To trace its history is to trace the arc of Western civilization's transformation from a world of manuscript culture to one of mass communication.\n\nThe story begins long before Johannes Gutenberg, with the invention of paper and the development of early printing techniques in East Asia. Papermaking, one of China's great contributions to the world, was developed around the 2nd century CE. By the 7th century, the Chinese had mastered woodblock printing, a technique where entire pages were carved into a single wooden block, inked, and pressed onto paper. The earliest surviving dated printed book, the Diamond Sutra, was produced in 868 CE, demonstrating a sophisticated understanding of reproduction technology. This method spread to Korea and Japan, where it was used extensively for Buddhist texts and later for secular works. In Korea, during",
