   "text": "The history of the printing press is not merely the history of a machine; it is the history of a revolution in human consciousness. Before the advent of movable type in fifteenth-century Europe, knowledge was a precious, scarce commodity, painstakingly preserved and transmitted through the labor of scribes. The printing press shattered that monopoly, democratizing information and setting in motion a cascade of transformations\u2014religious, political, scientific, and social\u2014that would ultimately give birth to the modern world. To trace its history is to trace the fault lines of modernity itself.\n\nThe story begins long before Johannes Gutenberg. The impulse to mechanize the reproduction of text is ancient. In China, woodblock printing was already in use by the seventh century during the Tang dynasty. Buddhist monks pressed inked paper onto carved wooden blocks to reproduce sutras, and by the ninth century, the first printed book\u2014the Diamond Sutra\u2014had been produced. This technology spread to Korea and Japan, where it was used for both religious and secular texts. The Chinese also developed movable type using baked clay and later wood and metal, with the inventor Bi Sheng credited around 1040 with creating the first known movable type system. However, the Chinese script's thousands of characters made movable type less practical than woodblock printing",
