   "text": "# The History of the Printing Press: From Woodblock to Mass Communication\n\nThe history of the printing press is, in many ways, the history of how knowledge became public. For most of human history, the written word was a scarce and expensive commodity, guarded by elites and produced slowly by hand. The invention and refinement of printing technologies over roughly two millennia transformed that reality, enabling the rapid spread of ideas, the standardization of languages, the rise of literacy, and the great upheavals of religion, science, and politics that shaped the modern world. To trace the history of the printing press is to trace the gradual democratization of information\u2014a process that began long before Johannes Gutenberg and continues to unfold in our own digital age.\n\nThe story begins not in fifteenth-century Europe but in ancient China. Woodblock printing, in which a carved wooden block is inked and pressed onto paper or cloth, emerged during the Tang dynasty (618\u2013907 CE). The earliest surviving dated printed document is a Buddhist scroll known as the Diamond Sutra, produced in 868 CE and discovered in the caves of Dunhuang. The technique involved carving entire pages in relief, inking the raised surfaces, and pressing paper against them\u2014a laborious process, but one that allowed for the",
